**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 17’s electrolysis and quantitative examples; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), electrolytic cells and electrolysis; [Química geral no laboratório — Marcela Boroski, organizer](../sources/Quimica-geral-no-laboratorio.lingua#r_48YEN2SA34DW0FPCSTMVTW3CTD), the complete electrochemistry activity.

Electrical charge passed is q_el = ∫I dt. For a specified electrode reaction transferring z electrons per reaction event, the ideal amount relation is n = q_el/(zF) on the chosen stoichiometric basis. Current efficiency accounts for the defined fraction producing a desired change; it needs a measurement and chemical-product account rather than a guessed correction.

For invented M = 63.5 g mol⁻¹, z = 2, constant current 2.00 A for 3600 s, and current efficiency 0.90, the desired deposited mass is about 2.132 g. This is a numerical Faraday-law exercise, not an instruction for a particular electrolyte or apparatus. Actual products depend on thermodynamics, kinetics, composition, transport, electrode material, and conditions.

Distinguish current efficiency, energy efficiency, conversion, recovery, and product purity. A high charge-to-product efficiency does not by itself imply low energy use or a pure stable product. Cell voltage under operation can exceed the reversible requirement through several physical contributions.

**Work:** derive the mass relation with units and independently check the example. Add a competing hypothetical electrode reaction and close the electron/element account. Explain what collected-product and electrical measurements would be needed to determine the selected efficiency and uncertainty.

Read the entire electrolysis treatment and the corresponding laboratory activity. Practical experiments require their appropriate supervised electrical/chemical arrangements and waste management. Later refining, electrochemical manufacture, batteries, and corrosion work will deepen the material-specific pathways and apparatus.

**Continue:** [Explain corrosion through coupled electrochemical and material processes](Corrosion-passivation-and-environment.lingua#r_5HRF141YG6H9FJ8F3HKNGCA4NC).

